  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08861v6</id>
    <title>Normalized Derivations for Steenrod--Milnor Operations on the Dickson Algebra and Applications</title>
    <updated>2026-08-06T07:02:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08861v6" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.08861v6" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Computing higher iterates of the primitive Steenrod-Milnor operations $\mathrm{St}^{\emptyset,Δ_i}$ on the Dickson algebra $D_n$ has historically been obstructed by severe combinatorial complexity. We resolve this for all $i \ge 1$ by introducing a normalized derivation $δ_i = (-1)^n Q_{n,0}^{-1} \mathrm{St}^{\emptyset,Δ_i}$. Starting from a recent explicit formula of Nguyen Sum, we show that $δ_i$ preserves $D_n$ and transforms the non-linear action into a tractable affine differential system. Resolving the non-commutativity of the operators via unsigned Stirling numbers of the first kind, we derive closed formulas for all higher iterates, systematically yielding the ambient nilpotence identity $(\mathrm{St}^{\emptyset,Δ_i})^p = 0$ on $D_n$. Furthermore, $δ_i$ satisfies a restricted $p$-power relation $δ_i^p = B^{p-1}δ_i$, where $B = R_{n,i}^p$. When $B \neq 0$, localizing at $B$ yields an explicit $\mathbb{F}_p$-weight decomposition of the localized Dickson algebra via Lagrange projectors, providing exact kernel and image descriptions for both operators. As applications, we explicitly determine the kernel and image of $δ_i$ in the classical range $1 \le i \le n$. This formalism unifies and upgrades the classical first-order formulas of Smith-Switzer and Wilkerson, along with Sum's low extra formulas ($i=n+1, n+2$), into closed expressions for all iterates. Finally, we construct a commutative-algebra Koszul complex from the normalized-ratio coefficients, clarifying its structural distinction from the square-zero Margolis complexes recently studied in the literature.</summary>
    <category term="math.AT" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-10T00:19:34Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>30 pages. In this final version, further references and new structural and computational results have been incorporated. Constructive comments and discussions are always appreciated</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="math.AT"/>
    <author>
      <name>Dang Vo Phuc</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08207v2</id>
    <title>Aurora: Architecting Argonne's First Exascale Supercomputer for Accelerated Scientific Discovery</title>
    <updated>2025-12-08T18:45:43Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08207v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.08207v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Aurora is Argonne National Laboratory's pioneering Exascale supercomputer, designed to accelerate scientific discovery with cutting-edge architectural innovations. Key new technologies include the Intel(TM) Xeon(TM) Data Center GPU Max Series (code-named Sapphire Rapids) with support for High Bandwidth Memory (HBM), alongside the Intel(TM) Data Center GPU Max Series (code-named Ponte Vecchio) on each compute node. Aurora also integrates the Distributed Asynchronous Object Storage (DAOS), a novel exascale storage solution, and leverages Intel's oneAPI programming environment. This paper presents an in-depth exploration of Aurora's node architecture, the HPE Slingshot interconnect, the supporting software ecosystem, and DAOS. We provide insights into standard benchmark performance and applications readiness efforts via Aurora's Early Science Program and the Exascale Computing Project.</summary>
    <category term="cs.DC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.PF" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-10T00:30:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>40 pages, 10 figures. Submitted to J. Supercomputing</arxiv:comment>
